[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-19338"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-20435"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=\\frac{1}{2}a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-275120"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-120244/selection/9b32bd1c2d85515e