cademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-70140/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70140"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-120260"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/73e0ac67304b5e7682abddb59fcd01a9e8aca88831a303bf5b31a72ff772228d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-3b=-3a-3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-325974"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/bae20e625c529ef2157faa4d534a19529c605c9002ed750e56723ff29e265226.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-4b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/139a4d4312ecbe42cd3a3859af4f4d7b93f8bbc54548ac5a0244c04e7ba5287b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-4b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-4b=-3a-4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-367059/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "9706540e-ce36-5d80-9a63-097023b2bead" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-367059/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a49739fd-5d64-5072-991b-0e2de77044c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-367059/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3e5725-87bb-5907-a975-4d9a4d0f162e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-367059/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-367059/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-367059/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-367059" :question/id #uuid "006dbbba-1a21-56cc-a22e-dea7b7e3886d" :question/math-academy-id "q-367059" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/9d117a1429a5d5264ca08bfae899086d821b37a84ec03921347f8e0fb207343f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/8daf3d00afbe4afe9678853f4fec50dd0861ca9a36a6dae96f8fa2d288c45be1.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-367059/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8"] :knowledg