 Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-367061/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-367061"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-367064"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/80cedeceb8d377617af880cab1c383643e3cd79055eccce046b0418598897ac8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/7e6923e8b8b774eeaa2f885ec6aa24bb03f07c547fa50e7145a4113a18e92e8a.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-2a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-326013"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-327074/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1881c0f3-cd34-5af6-b500-f3259283a978" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327074/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d014524-58d3-529c-a22b-f6b9b63acb49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327074/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-327074/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327074/field-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "cce72f04-5ebd-5154-bf57-a0ac41573377" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327074/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "59582d9e-7546-51c2-acfd-74c2eabbf30b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327074/field-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-327074/field-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327074" :question/id #uuid "126ed0a4-da79-50b6-9c3c-a0a12a9343df" :question/math-academy-id "q-327074" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-327074/field-1" "q-327074/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-327074"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-120279"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-42676/selection/8a58be6361f03ea07efb634109cd91972e9dc9a063473e3a89799fd73479a27b" :answer/id #uuid "8c197631-0385-5be5-8c82-bb1e66244c38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42676/selection/f735528f90686faa943f749393bc4b331adff89523a059c2e72b09217317442d" :answer/id #uuid "6c20faa9-4dc5-59cb-8962-551d32ed860c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42676/selection/62859331e5e202c4a541283879cd53e0dd2591278a08e6cf83d965f2ea0f2f7d" :answer/id #uuid "208efaab-2244-5ad9-ba54-968db33be116" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42676/selection/73080cf7d4482ffe183bd1db495ef56782f8d593c4a57d4038ee543b0bad40b6" :answer/id #uuid "0d41c933-e27f-5d90-aa62-553ba514bbf0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42676/selection/9c810e894744b6a2ef2c703c682898f4521a53161d72e1ee04cc9a83215928ec" :answer/id #uuid "8a2dea85-a020-55f5-b7dd-b26cdcc4c2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} 