 ["q-42676/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-42676"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-120268"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-120303"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=n$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-322586/field-1/2d5b329ffad8f2e6cddf3b1013d5c47f7be15c20228e40db178eef96670c9868" :answer/id #uuid "fb4bdfa5-e724-56eb-8a6a-3f16a9657d0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" 